.TH TRAJLABTEST 1
.SH NAME
trajlabtest \- check every Danby flight-path model written as a trajlab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trajlabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Trajlabtest
is to
.IR trajlab (2)
what
.IR poplabtest (1)
is to
.IR poplab (2):
it reads the
.B SPEC
constant out of every model under
.B /appl/danby
that calls
.BR trajlab->run ,
so that what is checked is the string that ships.
.PP
Every model must parse, must have the four states a flight has, must
have no NaN among its rates at the launch, and must satisfy the
kinematic identity that makes those four a position and a velocity
rather than four unrelated numbers: the first two rates are exactly the
last two states.
.PP
Every model whose air can be switched off also gets the vacuum check.
With the drag, the lift and the spin all set to zero, the horizontal
acceleration must be exactly zero and the vertical exactly minus
gravity, and the launch speed must be exactly the speed parameter.  That
is the strongest single statement one can make about a ballistic model
without integrating it, and it is checked exactly rather than within a
tolerance.
.PP
.B -v
lists each model with its parameter and state counts.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/trajlabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR poplabtest (1),
.IR mechlabtest (1),
.IR trajlab (2)
.SH BUGS
A default outside its own slider range is deliberately not an error: the
baseball and the knuckleball are pitched slightly downwards, so their
angle is negative while the slider runs from zero, and that is the model
as it ships.
.PP
Which parameter is the drag, which the lift and which the spin is a
table in this program rather than something the spec states, so a model
with a differently ordered parameter list would silently skip the vacuum
check.
